Describe a time when you felt proud of a family member

I'd like to talk about a time when I felt extremely proud of my cousin after he was accepted into two universities at the same time.

If I remember correctly, this happened about six years ago when I was in my final year of high school. One evening, I was lying on my bed and scrolling through social media when I suddenly received a message from him. In the message, he shared the exciting news that he had been accepted into his dream university, and even more impressively, he had also received an offer from another well-known institution.

As soon as I saw the message, I called him to congratulate him. He sounded incredibly excited and could hardly contain his happiness. During the conversation, he invited me to a celebration party that his family was organizing that weekend to mark this important achievement.

What made me especially proud was the effort he had put into reaching that goal. When he was younger, he was actually quite an average student. He loved spending time outdoors with his friends and wasn't particularly focused on his studies. However, everything changed when he developed an interest in engineering and decided that he wanted to attend a prestigious university.

From that point on, he became much more disciplined. He spent more time studying, reviewing lessons, and improving his academic skills. Besides attending regular classes, he also enrolled in extra courses to strengthen his knowledge and receive additional guidance from teachers. Over the years, his hard work paid off, and he consistently achieved excellent results in school.

I was not only happy for his success but also inspired by his determination. His journey taught me that talent alone is not enough; persistence and hard work are equally important. In fact, his success motivated me to work harder toward my own goals, and eventually, I was able to get into my dream university as well.

Looking back, that was one of the moments when I felt most proud of a family member. I'm fortunate to have someone like him in my life, and I still see him as a role model today.
